# NOTES
# Structural finding 1: "Atomstroyexport" as blanket constructor is anachronistic (founded 1998) for every Soviet-era unit in this batch built before ~1998 — i.e. all of Chernobyl, Ignalina, Khmelnytskyi-1, Rivne 1-3, all of South Ukraine, all of Zaporizhzhia, and both Armenian units. It is legitimately correct only where a documented post-1998 completion consortium is named: Khmelnytskyi-2 (completed 2000-2004) and Rivne-4 (completed 2006), both per World Nuclear Association's Ukraine country profile ("consortium of Framatome ANP and Atomstroyexport"). No specific pre-1998 Soviet construction-trust name could be confirmed for any unit this session (candidates like "Yuzhatomenergostroy" or "Zaporizhzhyaatomenergobud" were not found in sources checked) — flagged UNVERIFIABLE rather than guessed, per instructions.
# Structural finding 2: "Atomenergoproekt" as blanket architect-engineer is confirmed WRONG for Khmelnytskyi and Rivne — the actual general designer was Kyivenergoproekt (Kyiv Institute for Nuclear Power Plant Design), per its own Wikipedia page explicitly crediting both plants. Notably, that same source did NOT credit South Ukraine or Zaporizhzhia, so those two plants' architect-engineer remains genuinely unverifiable rather than assumed to also be Kyivenergoproekt.
# Structural finding 3: "Power Machines (LMZ)" as blanket turbine supplier is confirmed WRONG for VVER-1000 units — Ukrainian VVER-1000 plants (South Ukraine, Zaporizhzhia, and by strong inference Khmelnytskyi/Rivne-3/4) used Turboatom (Kharkiv Turbine Plant), not LMZ. This also applies to Chernobyl (RBMK) — Kharkiv Turbine Plant, not LMZ. Ignalina's existing "Turboatom" value was already correct. However, for the VVER-440 units at Rivne 1-2, the LMZ guess could not be confirmed OR refuted — LMZ was historically a real VVER-440 turbine supplier, so this is marked UNVERIFIABLE rather than WRONG (no unit-specific source found either way).
# Structural finding 4: "Izhora Plants" as blanket RPV manufacturer is demonstrably NOT plant-wide-safe even within a single site: South Ukraine Unit 1's vessel is confirmed Izhora, but Units 2 and 3 are confirmed Atommash (Volgodonsk) — a documented supplier switch as Atommash ramped up VVER-1000 vessel production in the early 1980s. Because of this precedent, the single plant-wide "Izhora" claim found for Zaporizhzhia (spanning 6 units built 1980-1996) was NOT accepted at face value and is marked UNVERIFIABLE per unit pending a unit-level source, rather than blanket-confirmed.
# Structural finding 5: Ignalina's constructor and architect-engineer fields are genuine data gaps, not resolvable from Wikipedia in Lithuanian/Russian/English — recommend a follow-up with Lithuanian state archives or Ignalina NPP's official decommissioning-history documents rather than accepting a guess. NIKIET (current architect_engineer guess) is confirmed to be a role misattribution regardless (it is the RBMK reactor designer, not the plant's general designer), but no replacement value could be sourced.
# Structural finding 6: Aktau/BN-350's "MAEC-Kaz" constructor value shows no evidence of existing as a real historical entity in any source checked (Wikipedia EN/RU, WNA, general web search) and appears to be a garbled conflation with "MAEK" (Mangystau Atomic Energy Combine), which is the plant's operating organization, not its builder. Recommend treating this as a likely-fabricated placeholder needing replacement from an archival source (e.g. IAEA's BN-350 decommissioning technical report, which could not be accessed this session — paywalled/403).
# Research constraint: WebSearch tool quota (200/session) was exhausted early in three of the four research sub-tasks (Ukrainian VVER cluster, RBMK cluster, Armenian NPP, Aktau), forcing a pivot to direct WebFetch of known Wikipedia/company-history URLs. This capped verification to sources reachable by direct URL guessing — IAEA PRIS unit-detail pages, WNA per-unit pages, and power-technology.com were largely inaccessible (wrong reactor IDs resolved to unrelated plants, 403/404/402 errors). A follow-up pass with a fresh WebSearch budget or direct IAEA NUCLEUS/INIS document access would likely resolve several of the UNVERIFIABLE entries above, particularly: pre-1998 Soviet construction-trust names, South Ukraine/Zaporizhzhia architect-engineer, Rivne VVER-440 turbine/RPV suppliers, and Ignalina constructor/architect-engineer.
